KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ const array: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [basic.type.qualifier]/3, [temp.deduct.type]/1: an array of const char
is "const T" with T = char[3].  The front end deduces T = const char[3],
so I<const char[3]> derives from itself and the type checker recurses
without bound in primary_base / vtable_pointer_component (SIGSEGV).
libstdc++'s incrementable_traits<const _Tp> : incrementable_traits<_Tp> takes
this path for the type of a string literal: the last base resolved before
the crash of cpp11_libstdcxx_header_drops/test_format.desc
(std::format("{}", 42)) is incrementable_traits<const char[1]> :
incrementable_traits<const char[1]>.  Pre-existing (bin53).  g++ 13 and
clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
